[38;5;231m⏺[39m Third fork, and the last one before I present the design: how the new module gets loaded, and whether we set up tests.
  These are coupled — what makes [38;5;153mpreferences.js[39m importable by a test runner is the same choice that decides how [38;5;153mapp.js[39m
  consumes it.

  Today [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a plain [38;5;153m<script src>[39m with everything in global scope; [38;5;153msrc/[39m uses CommonJS but is unrelated code the
  page never loads.

  - [1mES modules + [38;5;153mnode:test[0m — [38;5;153mpreferences.js[39m exports a store; [38;5;153mindex.html[39m switches to [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m. Node's
    built-in test runner (zero dependencies, already on your machine) imports the module directly and tests it against
    an in-memory [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m fake. Cost: ES modules don't load over [38;5;153mfile://[39m, so opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m by double-clicking
    stops working — you'd need [38;5;153mnpx serve[39m or similar.
  - [1mKeep script globals, no tests[0m — add a second [38;5;153m<script src="preferences.js">[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, exposing a global. Matches
    the current style exactly and keeps [38;5;153mfile://[39m working. Cost: nothing verifies the store except loading the page by
    hand.
  - [1mKeep script globals + [38;5;153mnode:test[0m — same loading, but the file needs a dual export shim (global in the browser,
    [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m in Node) so tests can reach it. Works, but the shim is boilerplate that exists purely for the test
    runner.

  I recommend ES modules + [38;5;153mnode:test[39m: it's still zero-dependency, and a storage layer is exactly the kind of code where
  an unnoticed serialization bug survives manual clicking. The [38;5;153mfile://[39m tradeoff is real though — if you open this page
  straight from disk, say so and I'll take the third option.
